1
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:10,041
Imagine being buried alive. Not for an hour. Not for a day.

2
00:00:11,141 --> 00:00:12,951
For one hundred days.

3
00:00:14,051 --> 00:00:15,461
No sunlight. No sky.

4
00:00:15,501 --> 00:00:19,350
No way of knowing whether it's morning or midnight.

5
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:21,959
Just concrete walls...

6
00:00:21,999 --> 00:00:24,917
a limited supply of food, water and air...

7
00:00:24,957 --> 00:00:27,853
and the silence of the earth above you.

8
00:00:28,953 --> 00:00:31,929
Could you stay sane? Could you stay alive?

9
00:00:33,029 --> 00:00:34,862
This is the story of Noah.

10
00:00:34,902 --> 00:00:39,473
And of the hundred days he spent inside an underground bunker...

11
00:00:39,513 --> 00:00:41,464
that nobody knew existed.

12
00:00:51,064 --> 00:00:53,868
It began as an ordinary job.

13
00:00:54,968 --> 00:01:00,832
Noah worked for a small engineering firm that inspected old buildings before they

14
00:01:00,872 --> 00:01:03,456
were demolished. Bridges. Factories.

15
00:01:03,496 --> 00:01:06,197
Tunnels nobody had used in decades.

16
00:01:07,297 --> 00:01:10,496
That Monday, his assignment was a strange one.

17
00:01:10,536 --> 00:01:15,425
A forgotten civil defense bunker, built in the early nineteen sixties,

18
00:01:15,465 --> 00:01:19,065
hidden in the hills forty miles outside the city.

19
00:01:20,165 --> 00:01:23,104
The land had been sold. A highway was coming.

20
00:01:23,144 --> 00:01:26,613
Someone needed to go down there, check the structure,

21
00:01:26,653 --> 00:01:30,709
and sign a report saying it was safe to fill with concrete.

22
00:01:31,809 --> 00:01:34,559
His colleague was supposed to come with him.

23
00:01:34,599 --> 00:01:38,871
But she called in sick that morning, and the deadline was Friday.

24
00:01:39,971 --> 00:01:43,767
It's just a hole in a hill. In and out. Two hours, tops.

25
00:01:44,867 --> 00:01:50,157
He didn't tell anyone exactly where he was going. It didn't seem important.

26
00:01:51,257 --> 00:01:55,552
Behind the rusted steel door, a staircase dropped into the dark.

27
00:01:55,592 --> 00:01:59,129
Ninety-two steps, straight down into the mountain.

28
00:02:00,229 --> 00:02:03,079
At the bottom, a long concrete corridor.

29
00:02:03,119 --> 00:02:06,764
Caged lights on the ceiling. Pipes along the walls.

30
00:02:06,804 --> 00:02:12,038
Painted arrows pointing to rooms with names like Decontamination, Supply,

31
00:02:12,078 --> 00:02:13,095
and Command.

32
00:02:14,195 --> 00:02:16,307
The air was cold and still.

33
00:02:16,347 --> 00:02:19,685
It smelled of dust, and metal, and time.

34
00:02:20,785 --> 00:02:22,823
Okay. Main corridor, section A.

35
00:02:22,863 --> 00:02:27,839
Some water damage on the ceiling. Structure looks... surprisingly solid.

36
00:02:28,939 --> 00:02:32,181
He had been underground for exactly forty minutes...

37
00:02:32,221 --> 00:02:34,202
when the floor began to move.

38
00:02:39,302 --> 00:02:41,742
At first, it was only a vibration.

39
00:02:41,782 --> 00:02:43,785
A low hum, deep in the rock.

40
00:02:43,825 --> 00:02:47,331
Then the lights in the ceiling began to swing.

41
00:02:48,431 --> 00:02:51,062
And then the whole mountain roared.

42
00:02:52,162 --> 00:02:54,743
Dust poured from the cracks above him.

43
00:02:54,783 --> 00:02:57,570
Somewhere behind him, near the staircase,

44
00:02:57,610 --> 00:03:01,002
came a sound like thunder trapped inside a box.

45
00:03:02,102 --> 00:03:03,356
No, no, no, no!

46
00:03:04,456 --> 00:03:07,179
Noah ran back toward the stairs, coughing,

47
00:03:07,219 --> 00:03:10,922
his flashlight cutting through a cloud of grey powder.

48
00:03:12,022 --> 00:03:15,901
When the dust finally settled... the staircase was gone.

49
00:03:15,941 --> 00:03:21,130
In its place, a wall of broken concrete and rock, from floor to ceiling.

50
00:03:25,230 --> 00:03:29,744
He pulled at the rocks until his fingers bled. They didn't move.

51
00:03:30,844 --> 00:03:33,688
Help! Can anyone hear me? Help! I'm down here!

52
00:03:34,788 --> 00:03:39,332
His voice died against the stone. He checked his phone. No signal.

53
00:03:39,372 --> 00:03:41,415
Of course there was no signal.

54
00:03:41,455 --> 00:03:44,314
He was thirty meters inside a mountain.

55
00:03:45,414 --> 00:03:49,150
It was later reported as a magnitude six earthquake.

56
00:03:49,190 --> 00:03:52,853
It damaged hundreds of buildings across the region.

57
00:03:52,893 --> 00:03:55,584
Thankfully, almost nobody was hurt.

58
00:03:56,684 --> 00:03:58,318
Almost nobody.

59
00:03:59,418 --> 00:04:05,143
In all the chaos, no one noticed that a young engineer hadn't come back from a site

60
00:04:05,183 --> 00:04:06,653
visit in the hills.

61
00:04:11,753 --> 00:04:13,340
For the first few hours,

62
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:18,965
Noah sat with his back against the rubble and did nothing at all. He just breathed.

63
00:04:19,005 --> 00:04:19,968
In. And out.

64
00:04:21,068 --> 00:04:26,543
Then the engineer in him took over. Panic wouldn't move rocks. A plan might.

65
00:04:27,643 --> 00:04:27,928
Okay.

66
00:04:27,968 --> 00:04:30,334
Rule one: don't waste the flashlight.

67
00:04:30,374 --> 00:04:34,815
Rule two: find out what this place has. Rule three... don't panic.

68
00:04:35,915 --> 00:04:41,229
At the end of the corridor, behind a heavy door, he found the generator room.

69
00:04:41,269 --> 00:04:46,217
An old diesel engine, the size of a car, still sitting on its mounts.

70
00:04:47,317 --> 00:04:50,176
He checked the oil. He checked the fuel.

71
00:04:50,216 --> 00:04:54,134
He pulled the starter cord once. Twice. Seven times.

72
00:04:55,234 --> 00:04:59,213
On the eighth pull, the engine coughed... and came to life.

73
00:05:03,313 --> 00:05:05,916
For the first time, the bunker was lit.

74
00:05:05,956 --> 00:05:08,613
And it was bigger than he'd imagined.

75
00:05:09,713 --> 00:05:14,733
There was a storage room, lined with shelves of canned food, water drums,

76
00:05:14,773 --> 00:05:19,567
and medical supplies, all stamped with the year nineteen sixty-two.

77
00:05:20,667 --> 00:05:23,124
There was a water tank, half full.

78
00:05:23,164 --> 00:05:27,017
A hand-cranked air filtration machine. Six bunk beds.

79
00:05:27,057 --> 00:05:31,907
And in the command room, an old military radio, covered in dust.

80
00:05:33,007 --> 00:05:38,709
The bunker had been built to keep twelve people alive for two weeks after a nuclear

81
00:05:38,749 --> 00:05:38,985
war.

82
00:05:39,025 --> 00:05:43,257
Now it had to keep one person alive for as long as it took.

83
00:05:44,357 --> 00:05:48,027
He did the math on the back of his inspection report.

84
00:05:48,067 --> 00:05:50,618
The fuel would last maybe sixty days,

85
00:05:50,658 --> 00:05:54,098
if he ran the generator only a few hours a day.

86
00:05:55,198 --> 00:06:01,155
The water, maybe ninety, if he was careful. The food, longer. Most of it, anyway.

87
00:06:02,255 --> 00:06:03,297
Sixty days. Okay.

88
00:06:03,337 --> 00:06:06,732
Someone will come long before that. Someone has to.

89
00:06:11,832 --> 00:06:16,359
On the first day, he was certain he would be rescued by nightfall.

90
00:06:17,459 --> 00:06:21,230
On the second day, he was certain they would come tomorrow.

91
00:06:22,330 --> 00:06:27,016
He spent hours in the command room, turning the dial of the old radio,

92
00:06:27,056 --> 00:06:29,164
listening for anything human.

93
00:06:30,264 --> 00:06:32,152
There was only static.

94
00:06:33,252 --> 00:06:38,260
He found a ventilation shaft that rose through the rock toward the surface,

95
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:41,882
and he shouted up into it until his voice gave out.

96
00:06:42,982 --> 00:06:47,813
Hello? Is anyone up there? My name is Noah! I'm trapped in the bunker! Please!

97
00:06:48,913 --> 00:06:54,170
Far above, he could see a tiny circle of grey light. Thirty meters of rock.

98
00:06:54,210 --> 00:06:56,549
And a steel grate, rusted shut.

99
00:06:57,649 --> 00:07:02,312
He told himself that his company would notice. That his sister would call.

100
00:07:02,352 --> 00:07:05,744
That someone would find his car parked by the road.

101
00:07:06,844 --> 00:07:11,201
What he didn't know was that a landslide, triggered by the earthquake,

102
00:07:11,241 --> 00:07:14,658
had buried the entrance road... and his car with it.

103
00:07:15,758 --> 00:07:19,832
Up on the surface, the police had opened a missing person case.

104
00:07:19,872 --> 00:07:21,981
But nobody knew where to look.

105
00:07:23,081 --> 00:07:25,163
On the fifth day, Noah heard it.

106
00:07:25,203 --> 00:07:30,535
A deep, rhythmic thudding, somewhere above him. Boom. Boom. Boom. Like machinery.

107
00:07:30,575 --> 00:07:32,118
Like someone digging.

108
00:07:33,218 --> 00:07:36,164
They're here! They found me! I'm here! I'm down here!

109
00:07:37,264 --> 00:07:42,413
He banged a wrench against the pipes for an hour. Then two. The thudding stopped.

110
00:07:42,453 --> 00:07:43,630
It never came back.

111
00:07:43,670 --> 00:07:46,897
Much later, he would learn it had been a road crew,

112
00:07:46,937 --> 00:07:50,803
clearing the landslide on a highway three kilometers away.

113
00:07:51,903 --> 00:07:57,012
By the end of the first week, Noah had stopped expecting rescue every hour.

114
00:07:57,052 --> 00:08:00,703
Now he simply hoped for it. Quietly. Like a prayer.

115
00:08:05,803 --> 00:08:10,463
The hardest thing to ration wasn't food, or water. It was light.

116
00:08:11,563 --> 00:08:16,172
To save fuel, Noah ran the generator for only four hours a day.

117
00:08:16,212 --> 00:08:19,863
The other twenty, he lived in near-total darkness,

118
00:08:19,903 --> 00:08:23,595
with a single flashlight and a box of batteries.

119
00:08:24,695 --> 00:08:29,100
And without the sun, something strange began to happen to time.

120
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:32,374
His body forgot when to sleep.

121
00:08:32,414 --> 00:08:35,178
Sometimes he slept for fourteen hours.

122
00:08:35,218 --> 00:08:38,836
Sometimes he lay awake for what felt like days.

123
00:08:39,936 --> 00:08:42,438
Scientists call it circadian drift.

124
00:08:42,478 --> 00:08:46,797
In experiments where people live underground without clocks,

125
00:08:46,837 --> 00:08:51,518
their sense of a day slowly stretches. Twenty-five hours. Thirty.

126
00:08:51,558 --> 00:08:53,161
Sometimes even more.

127
00:08:54,261 --> 00:08:56,395
Noah's watch told him the date.

128
00:08:56,435 --> 00:08:59,039
But his body no longer believed it.

129
00:09:00,139 --> 00:09:03,438
Day sixteen. Or seventeen. I keep losing count.

130
00:09:03,478 --> 00:09:07,984
I scratch a line on the wall every time the watch says midnight.

131
00:09:08,024 --> 00:09:11,158
It's the only thing that still feels real.

132
00:09:12,258 --> 00:09:14,274
He began talking to the bunker.

133
00:09:14,314 --> 00:09:16,264
He named the generator Bertha.

134
00:09:16,304 --> 00:09:20,433
He apologized to the air filter when he cranked it too hard.

135
00:09:21,533 --> 00:09:26,809
And every so often, in the silence, he heard things. A tapping in the pipes.

136
00:09:26,849 --> 00:09:30,637
Footsteps in the corridor. A voice calling his name.

137
00:09:31,737 --> 00:09:33,755
There was never anyone there.

138
00:09:33,795 --> 00:09:39,196
In total isolation, the human brain begins to fill the silence on its own.

139
00:09:40,296 --> 00:09:41,084
He knew that.

140
00:09:41,124 --> 00:09:44,458
It didn't make the footsteps any less frightening.

141
00:09:49,558 --> 00:09:54,376
On the twenty-third day, while searching the command room for spare fuses,

142
00:09:54,416 --> 00:09:57,520
Noah found a metal box bolted under the desk.

143
00:09:58,620 --> 00:10:00,261
Inside was a logbook.

144
00:10:00,301 --> 00:10:05,655
Its cover read: Station Officer, Walter Hale. Nineteen sixty-two.

145
00:10:06,755 --> 00:10:10,957
Noah opened it under the flashlight, and began to read.

146
00:10:12,057 --> 00:10:12,620
Day one.

147
00:10:12,660 --> 00:10:15,184
The drill began at oh six hundred.

148
00:10:15,224 --> 00:10:19,407
They sealed the doors behind me. One hundred days alone,

149
00:10:19,447 --> 00:10:25,667
to prove that a man can survive down here if the worst ever happens. I volunteered.

150
00:10:25,707 --> 00:10:27,742
My wife thinks I'm crazy.

151
00:10:28,842 --> 00:10:31,566
Walter had been a civil defense officer.

152
00:10:31,606 --> 00:10:36,612
The bunker had been tested with a single volunteer, for one hundred days,

153
00:10:36,652 --> 00:10:38,875
sealed from the outside world.

154
00:10:39,975 --> 00:10:44,278
Noah read the journal slowly. One entry a day. Never more.

155
00:10:44,318 --> 00:10:47,239
It became the best part of every day.

156
00:10:48,339 --> 00:10:49,211
Day nineteen.

157
00:10:49,251 --> 00:10:53,000
Lost track of whether it is day or night. Slept badly.

158
00:10:53,040 --> 00:10:58,734
Heard footsteps in the corridor again. There was no one. There is never anyone.

159
00:10:59,834 --> 00:11:01,507
Me too, Walter. Me too.

160
00:11:02,607 --> 00:11:03,653
Day thirty-one.

161
00:11:03,693 --> 00:11:07,634
Talked to the generator today. I have named her Bertha.

162
00:11:07,674 --> 00:11:11,009
If anyone ever reads this, I am not ashamed.

163
00:11:12,109 --> 00:11:15,000
Noah laughed for the first time in a month.

164
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:18,326
A real laugh, loud, echoing down the corridor.

165
00:11:19,426 --> 00:11:21,970
Day forty. Margaret would laugh at me.

166
00:11:22,010 --> 00:11:26,865
I have started to make the bed every morning, though no one will see it.

167
00:11:26,905 --> 00:11:31,475
A man needs a reason to stand up straight, even under a mountain.

168
00:11:32,575 --> 00:11:34,924
So Noah began making his bed too.

169
00:11:34,964 --> 00:11:37,864
Every morning, whatever morning meant.

170
00:11:38,964 --> 00:11:44,379
Sixty years apart, two men had sat in the same chair, under the same mountain,

171
00:11:44,419 --> 00:11:46,387
fighting the same silence.

172
00:11:47,487 --> 00:11:51,571
And somehow, Noah didn't feel completely alone anymore.

173
00:11:56,671 --> 00:11:59,529
On day forty-four, Bertha stopped.

174
00:12:03,029 --> 00:12:08,248
The lights died. The air machine went quiet. And the darkness was total.

175
00:12:09,348 --> 00:12:11,846
Noah found the problem by flashlight.

176
00:12:11,886 --> 00:12:15,344
A cracked fuel line, leaking diesel onto the floor.

177
00:12:15,384 --> 00:12:20,472
Every hour it stayed broken, he was losing fuel he would never get back.

178
00:12:21,572 --> 00:12:24,329
He had no spare parts. No proper tools.

179
00:12:24,369 --> 00:12:27,269
Only a toolbox from nineteen sixty-two...

180
00:12:27,309 --> 00:12:30,113
and the instructions in his own head.

181
00:12:31,213 --> 00:12:32,747
It took him nine hours.

182
00:12:32,787 --> 00:12:37,536
He patched the line with a piece of rubber hose, wire from a bunk bed,

183
00:12:37,576 --> 00:12:40,258
and a strip of his own jacket lining.

184
00:12:41,358 --> 00:12:43,312
Then he pulled the cord.

185
00:12:44,412 --> 00:12:45,469
Nothing.

186
00:12:46,569 --> 00:12:48,503
He pulled again. And again.

187
00:12:48,543 --> 00:12:51,837
Until his arm shook and his hands were raw.

188
00:12:52,937 --> 00:12:54,330
Come on, Bertha. Please.

189
00:12:54,370 --> 00:12:57,207
Please, I can't do this in the dark. I can't.

190
00:12:58,307 --> 00:13:03,160
He sat down on the cold floor and, for the first time since the collapse,

191
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:04,087
Noah cried.

192
00:13:05,187 --> 00:13:07,615
Not for the darkness. For his sister.

193
00:13:07,655 --> 00:13:10,749
For the phone call he'd missed the week before.

194
00:13:10,789 --> 00:13:14,140
For every ordinary morning he had never noticed.

195
00:13:15,240 --> 00:13:18,369
When the tears stopped, he picked up the flashlight,

196
00:13:18,409 --> 00:13:22,155
checked the patch one more time... and found a loose clamp.

197
00:13:23,255 --> 00:13:24,796
One more pull.

198
00:13:28,496 --> 00:13:30,333
Bertha roared back to life.

199
00:13:30,373 --> 00:13:35,460
And Noah laughed and cried at the same time, alone, under the mountain.

200
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:41,319
But the leak had cost him almost two weeks of fuel. The math had changed.

201
00:13:41,359 --> 00:13:43,882
He couldn't wait to be rescued anymore.

202
00:13:43,922 --> 00:13:46,110
He had to find another way out.

203
00:13:51,210 --> 00:13:56,919
At the end of the lowest corridor was a door Noah had avoided since the first day.

204
00:13:56,959 --> 00:13:59,843
A blast door, painted yellow and black.

205
00:14:00,943 --> 00:14:04,093
Level Two. Authorized personnel only.

206
00:14:05,193 --> 00:14:08,657
Walter's journal had mentioned it only once.

207
00:14:09,757 --> 00:14:10,422
Day sixty.

208
00:14:10,462 --> 00:14:15,852
Opened the lower level today, as instructed, to inspect the emergency tunnel.

209
00:14:15,892 --> 00:14:17,263
Partially collapsed.

210
00:14:17,303 --> 00:14:22,389
The engineers said it would not be needed. Let us hope they are right.

211
00:14:23,489 --> 00:14:24,996
An emergency tunnel.

212
00:14:25,036 --> 00:14:27,585
Noah read the line three times.

213
00:14:28,685 --> 00:14:32,772
The wheel on the blast door hadn't turned in sixty years.

214
00:14:32,812 --> 00:14:37,161
It took him two days, a crowbar, and most of a can of oil.

215
00:14:38,261 --> 00:14:43,023
When it finally opened, a breath of cold air rolled out of the dark.

216
00:14:44,123 --> 00:14:47,867
Behind it, he found a small room, a ladder going down,

217
00:14:47,907 --> 00:14:50,319
and a framed blueprint on the wall.

218
00:14:50,359 --> 00:14:52,471
The full plan of the bunker.

219
00:14:53,571 --> 00:14:57,954
And at the bottom of the plan, a narrow passage, marked in red,

220
00:14:57,994 --> 00:15:02,938
leading away from the bunker and up toward the surface. Emergency exit.

221
00:15:02,978 --> 00:15:05,726
Length: one hundred and forty meters.

222
00:15:06,826 --> 00:15:11,175
Noah climbed down the ladder and shined his light into the tunnel.

223
00:15:12,275 --> 00:15:14,296
The first stretch was intact.

224
00:15:14,336 --> 00:15:19,057
Then, about thirty meters in, it ended in a wall of dirt and stone.

225
00:15:19,097 --> 00:15:23,369
The collapse Walter had written about. Or maybe a new one.

226
00:15:24,469 --> 00:15:28,547
One hundred and forty meters. Okay. One meter at a time.

227
00:15:33,647 --> 00:15:37,360
In the supply room, he found two shovels, a pickaxe,

228
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:40,031
and a wheelbarrow with one flat tire.

229
00:15:40,071 --> 00:15:42,531
Equipment meant for emergencies.

230
00:15:43,631 --> 00:15:45,716
This was an emergency.

231
00:15:46,816 --> 00:15:48,751
Noah began to dig.

232
00:15:49,851 --> 00:15:55,086
He dug during the four hours of generator light, and rested during the dark.

233
00:15:55,126 --> 00:15:59,856
He moved the dirt into the empty rooms, one wheelbarrow at a time.

234
00:16:00,956 --> 00:16:04,866
He propped the ceiling with planks from the old bunk beds,

235
00:16:04,906 --> 00:16:07,931
the way he'd seen in drawings of mine shafts.

236
00:16:07,971 --> 00:16:10,179
Every meter, he checked the roof.

237
00:16:10,219 --> 00:16:12,889
Every meter, he listened to the rock.

238
00:16:13,989 --> 00:16:16,170
Some days he moved two meters.

239
00:16:16,210 --> 00:16:18,687
Some days, only a few centimeters,

240
00:16:18,727 --> 00:16:22,579
when he hit stone and had to chip it away by hand.

241
00:16:23,679 --> 00:16:26,101
Day seventy-four. Forty meters done.

242
00:16:26,141 --> 00:16:28,836
My hands don't open all the way anymore.

243
00:16:28,876 --> 00:16:30,888
Walter, if you're listening...

244
00:16:30,928 --> 00:16:34,497
I think I finally understand why you kept writing.

245
00:16:35,597 --> 00:16:36,761
His body changed.

246
00:16:36,801 --> 00:16:40,230
He grew thinner, and stronger. His beard grew in.

247
00:16:40,270 --> 00:16:43,748
His skin turned pale from the lack of sunlight.

248
00:16:44,848 --> 00:16:47,922
He took vitamin tablets from the medical kit,

249
00:16:47,962 --> 00:16:53,370
rationed his water to two liters a day, and counted every can of food twice.

250
00:16:54,470 --> 00:16:58,858
On day eighty-one, part of the tunnel ceiling came down behind him.

251
00:16:58,898 --> 00:17:02,485
For a terrible minute, he was trapped inside a trap.

252
00:17:03,585 --> 00:17:07,457
He dug himself out with his bare hands. Then he went back...

253
00:17:07,497 --> 00:17:10,451
and propped the ceiling twice as carefully.

254
00:17:11,551 --> 00:17:15,528
Ninety meters. One hundred. One hundred and ten.

255
00:17:16,628 --> 00:17:17,345
Day ninety.

256
00:17:17,385 --> 00:17:21,613
You know what I miss most, Walter? Not food. Not my bed. Rain.

257
00:17:21,653 --> 00:17:25,537
The sound of rain on a window. I used to hate rainy days.

258
00:17:25,577 --> 00:17:27,792
I'd give anything for one now.

259
00:17:28,892 --> 00:17:32,209
And then, on day ninety-six, the fuel ran out.

260
00:17:32,249 --> 00:17:36,413
Bertha coughed one last time, and fell silent for good.

261
00:17:37,513 --> 00:17:40,306
From now on, he would dig by flashlight.

262
00:17:40,346 --> 00:17:42,692
With the last of the batteries.

263
00:17:47,792 --> 00:17:53,414
On day ninety-nine, Noah opened Walter's journal to the last written page.

264
00:17:54,514 --> 00:17:55,616
Day ninety-nine.

265
00:17:55,656 --> 00:17:57,686
Tomorrow they open the doors.

266
00:17:57,726 --> 00:18:03,397
I have been afraid of many things down here. The dark. The silence. My own mind.

267
00:18:03,437 --> 00:18:06,514
But tonight I am afraid of something new.

268
00:18:07,614 --> 00:18:11,607
I am afraid that the world up there has gone on without me.

269
00:18:11,647 --> 00:18:17,281
That I will climb the stairs and find that nothing waited. But I will go up anyway.

270
00:18:17,321 --> 00:18:18,702
Tomorrow, I go up.

271
00:18:19,802 --> 00:18:23,875
Noah closed the book and sat in the dark for a long time.

272
00:18:24,975 --> 00:18:29,076
He had read the journal one page a day, for seventy-six days.

273
00:18:29,116 --> 00:18:31,248
He had never once skipped ahead.

274
00:18:31,288 --> 00:18:33,746
And now, there were no more pages.

275
00:18:34,846 --> 00:18:36,488
Only one thing remained.

276
00:18:36,528 --> 00:18:40,764
On the inside of the back cover, scratched faintly in pencil,

277
00:18:40,804 --> 00:18:43,898
was a final line he hadn't noticed before.

278
00:18:44,998 --> 00:18:48,077
Day one hundred. The sky is still there.

279
00:18:49,177 --> 00:18:51,982
That night, his flashlight began to fade.

280
00:18:52,022 --> 00:18:55,035
He was one hundred and thirty-two meters in.

281
00:18:55,075 --> 00:18:58,694
Eight meters from the end of the plan. Maybe less.

282
00:18:59,794 --> 00:19:02,228
Just eight more meters, Walter. Just eight.

283
00:19:07,328 --> 00:19:12,127
On the morning of day one hundred, Noah dug in total darkness.

284
00:19:13,227 --> 00:19:16,006
By feel. By sound. By memory.

285
00:19:17,106 --> 00:19:20,824
The tunnel had been climbing for the last twenty meters.

286
00:19:20,864 --> 00:19:26,584
The dirt above him was softer now. Damp. It smelled different. It smelled of roots.

287
00:19:27,684 --> 00:19:30,954
He pushed the shovel up into the ceiling one more time...

288
00:19:30,994 --> 00:19:33,119
and it broke through into nothing.

289
00:19:34,219 --> 00:19:37,223
A thin line of light fell into the tunnel.

290
00:19:38,323 --> 00:19:39,129
Real light.

291
00:19:39,169 --> 00:19:43,471
Gold and warm and moving, the way only sunlight moves.

292
00:19:48,071 --> 00:19:52,851
Noah clawed at the earth with both hands until the hole was wide enough...

293
00:19:52,891 --> 00:19:56,949
and he pulled himself up, out of the ground, into the world.

294
00:19:58,049 --> 00:20:03,249
He was in a forest, on the far side of the mountain. It was early morning.

295
00:20:03,289 --> 00:20:04,594
Birds were singing.

296
00:20:04,634 --> 00:20:07,192
Mist was rising between the trees.

297
00:20:08,292 --> 00:20:13,194
He lay on his back in the wet grass and stared upward, and for a long time,

298
00:20:13,234 --> 00:20:14,570
he couldn't speak.

299
00:20:15,670 --> 00:20:18,368
The sky is still there, Walter. It's still there.

300
00:20:19,468 --> 00:20:22,951
Two hours later, a forestry worker found a thin,

301
00:20:22,991 --> 00:20:28,456
bearded man sitting by an old logging road, wrapped in a torn green jacket,

302
00:20:28,496 --> 00:20:30,040
smiling at the sun.

303
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:33,408
Hey! Hey, are you okay? Where did you come from?

304
00:20:34,508 --> 00:20:37,155
From under the mountain. It's a long story.

305
00:20:38,255 --> 00:20:40,597
That afternoon, in a hospital bed,

306
00:20:40,637 --> 00:20:45,201
he made the phone call he'd been rehearsing for a hundred days.

307
00:20:46,301 --> 00:20:48,928
Noah? Oh my God. Noah, is that you?

308
00:20:50,028 --> 00:20:52,364
Hey, sis. Sorry I missed your call.

309
00:20:53,464 --> 00:20:59,526
Doctors later said he had lost eleven kilos and was severely deficient in vitamin D.

310
00:20:59,566 --> 00:21:02,868
But otherwise, he was in remarkable condition,

311
00:21:02,908 --> 00:21:06,908
for a man who had spent one hundred days underground.

312
00:21:08,008 --> 00:21:13,790
When reporters asked how he had survived, he gave them the same answer every time.

313
00:21:13,830 --> 00:21:17,388
He had rules. He had a plan. And he had company.

314
00:21:18,488 --> 00:21:22,101
The bunker was filled with concrete a few months later.

315
00:21:22,141 --> 00:21:25,886
But before it was sealed, Noah went back one last time...

316
00:21:25,926 --> 00:21:29,938
and left Walter's journal on the desk, open to the last page.

317
00:21:29,978 --> 00:21:33,714
With one new line, in his own handwriting, underneath.

318
00:21:34,814 --> 00:21:37,380
Day one hundred. It still is.

319
00:21:38,480 --> 00:21:43,478
Because sometimes, the only way out... is through. One meter at a time.

320
00:21:49,578 --> 00:21:52,655
Could you survive one hundred days underground?

321
00:21:52,695 --> 00:21:57,297
What would break you first: the darkness, the silence, or the waiting?

322
00:21:57,337 --> 00:21:59,079
Tell us in the comments.

323
00:22:00,179 --> 00:22:01,223
And remember...

324
00:22:01,263 --> 00:22:04,231
when everything ends, the story begins.
